<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · c. 1550 · Islamic Caliphates &amp; Middle East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Lekha Dodi</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="he" dir="rtl">לכה דודי</bdi> / <bdi lang="he-Latn">Lekha Dodi</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Hebrew song sung on Friday evenings to welcome the Sabbath as if greeting a bride, written about 500 years ago.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Solomon Alkabetz</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (hundreds of melodies)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Jewish communities; the Kabbalists of Safed</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hebrew</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Safed, present-day Israel</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>16th century</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Solomon Alkabetz (16th century); melodies traditional)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lekha-dodi">Lekha Dodi</a> is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> liturgical song sung in the synagogue on Friday at dusk, usually at sundown, to welcome the Sabbath before the evening service. It is part of Kabbalat Shabbat, the service of welcoming the Sabbath. It was composed in the 16th century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> Alkabetz, who was born in the Ottoman city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a> and became a leading Kabbalist of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safed">Safed</a>. As was common at the time, the poem is an acrostic: the first letters of its first eight stanzas spell the author's name. It is one of the latest Hebrew poems to be regularly accepted into the traditional liturgy.</p><p>Its refrain takes a phrase from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-of-songs">Song of Songs</a> (7:12), which the Talmudic sage Rava (Abba ben Joseph bar Ḥama) read, in Tractate Eruvin 21b, as the people of Israel speaking to God. Alkabetz drew on the rabbinic reading of the Song of Songs, in which the maiden stands for the people of Israel and the lover for God, and on the books of the Prophets, which use the same image. The poem asks God to bring the great Sabbath of messianic deliverance.</p><p>The song carries its own customs. During the last verse the whole congregation rises and turns to the west, or to the door, and some leave the sanctuary; at the words greeting the bride they bow and turn back to the front, and traditional congregations face <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> for the rest of the service. Practice varies: many Reform congregations leave out the verses about messianic redemption, Sephardic congregations of the Jerusalem and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> rites leave out the verses that speak of suffering, and the Sephardic rite and Hasidic tradition use a different last section.</p><p>Few liturgical texts have been sung to so many tunes. Early in the 20th century Abraham Zevi Idelsohn recorded hundreds. Some Sephardic congregations chant it to an old Moorish melody far older than the text: old prayer books direct it to be sung to the tune of a composition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> Halevi, who died nearly five centuries before Alkabetz, a tune brought to the Land of Israel by refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. In some old-style <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkenazi-jews">Ashkenazi</a> synagogues the cantor chants the verses at length and the congregation answers with the refrain; in most Ashkenazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> synagogues everyone sings it together. Northern European congregations kept two older tunes for parts of the year: one for the Omer weeks between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a> and Shavuot, which has been compared to &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lillibullero">Lillibullero</a>&quot; and to Mozart's Marriage of Figaro and resembles late 17th-century German folk song, and one for the Three Weeks before Tisha b'Av. A variant of the second was used by Benedetto Marcello in his setting of Psalm 19 (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, 1724) as an air of the German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, and the cantor Eduard Birnbaum traced it to a Polish folk song printed by Oskar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolobrzeg">Kolberg</a> in 1857. Another old melody, sung in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">City of London</a>'s synagogues, was used by Isaac Nathan for &quot;She Walks in Beauty&quot; in his setting of Byron's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> Melodies (1815).</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A mystical poem that welcomes the Sabbath as a bride, with the congregation and God going out together to meet her. Its stanzas move from the holiness of the Sabbath to a call for the restoration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and the people of Israel and the coming of messianic deliverance. The mood is joyful and expectant.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Hebrew</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Hebrew</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">לְכָה דוֹדִי לִקְרַאת כַּלָּה</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">lekhah dodi liqrat kallah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Come, my beloved, to meet the bride;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">פְּנֵי שַׁבָּת נְקַבְּלָה</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">pene shabbat neqabbelah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>let us welcome the face of the Sabbath.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">שָׁמוֹר וְזָכוֹר בְּדִבּוּר אֶחָד</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">shamor vezakhor bedibbur ekhad</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;Keep&quot; and &quot;Remember&quot; in a single utterance</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">הִשְׁמִיעָֽנוּ אֵל הַמְיֻחָד</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">hishmianu el hameyukhad</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the one God made us hear;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">יְיָ אֶחָד וּשְׁמוֹ אֶחָד</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">Adonai ekhad ushemo ekhad</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the Lord is one and his name is one,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">לְשֵׁם וּלְתִפְאֶֽרֶת וְלִתְהִלָּה</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">leshem uletiferet velithillah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for renown and for glory and for praise.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">לִקְרַאת שַׁבָּת לְכוּ וְנֵלְכָה</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">liqrat shabbat lekhu venelkhah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To meet the Sabbath come, let us go,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">כִּי הִיא מְקוֹר הַבְּרָכָה</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">ki hi meqor habberakhah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for she is the source of blessing;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">מֵרֹאשׁ מִקֶּֽדֶם נְסוּכָה</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">merosh miqqedem nesukhah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>from the beginning, from of old, she was set up [?];</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">סוֹף מַעֲשֶׂה בְּמַחֲשָׁבָה תְּחִלָּה</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">sof maaseh bemakhashavah tekhillah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>last in deed, first in thought.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">מִקְדַּשׁ מֶֽלֶךְ עִיר מְלוּכָה</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">miqdash melekh ir melukhah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Sanctuary of the King, royal city,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">קֽוּמִי צְאִי מִתּוֹךְ הַהֲפֵכָה</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">qumi tsei mittokh hahafekhah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>rise, go out from the overthrow;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">רַב לָךְ שֶֽׁבֶת בְּעֵֽמֶק הַבָּכָא</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">rav lakh shevet beemeq habakha</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>long enough have you sat in the valley of weeping,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וְהוּא יַחֲמֹל עָלַֽיִךְ חֶמְלָה</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">vehu yakhamol alayikh khemlah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and he will have compassion on you.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">הִתְנַעֲרִי מֵעָפָר קֽוּמִי</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">hitnaari meafar qumi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Shake yourself from the dust, rise;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">לִבְשִׁי בִּגְדֵי תִפְאַרְתֵּךְ עַמִּי</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">livshi bigde tifartekh ammi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>put on the garments of your glory, my people;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">עַל יַד בֶּן יִשַׁי בֵּית הַלַּחְמִי</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">al yad ben yishai bet hallakhmi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>by the hand of the son of Jesse the Bethlehemite</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">קׇרְבָה אֶל נַפְשִׁי גְּאָלָהּ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">qorvah el nafshi gealah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>draw near to my soul, redeem it.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">הִתְעוֹרְרִי הִתְעוֹרְרִי</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">hitoreri hitoreri</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Rouse yourself, rouse yourself,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">כִּי בָא אוֹרֵךְ קֽוּמִי אֽוֹרִי</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">ki va orekh qumi ori</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for your light has come; rise, shine;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">עֽוּרִי עֽוּרִי שִׁיר דַּבֵּֽרִי</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">uri uri shir dabberi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>awake, awake, utter a song;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">כְּבוֹד יְיָ עָלַֽיִךְ נִגְלָה</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">kevod Adonai alayikh niglah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the glory of the Lord is revealed upon you.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">לֹא תֵבֹֽשִׁי וְלֹא תִכָּלְמִי</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">lo tevoshi velo tikkalmi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You shall not be ashamed nor be disgraced;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">מַה תִּשְׁתּוֹחֲחִי וּמַה תֶּהֱמִי</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">mah tishtokhakhi umah tehemi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>why are you cast down, and why do you moan?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">בָּךְ יֶחֱסוּ עֲנִיֵּי עַמִּי</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">bakh yekhesu aniyye ammi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In you the poor of my people will take refuge,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וְנִבְנְתָה עִיר עַל תִּלָּהּ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">venivnetah ir al tillah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and the city will be rebuilt on its mound.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וְהָיוּ לִמְשִׁסָּה שֹׁאסָֽיִךְ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">vehayu limshissah shosayikh</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Those who plunder you shall become plunder,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וְרָחֲקוּ כׇּל מְבַלְּעָֽיִךְ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">verakhaqu kol mevalleayikh</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and all who would swallow you shall be far away;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">יָשִׂישׂ עָלַֽיִךְ אֱלֹהָֽיִךְ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">yasis alayikh elohayikh</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>your God shall rejoice over you</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">כִּמְשׂוֹשׂ חָתָן עַל כַּלָּה</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">kimsos khatan al kallah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>as a bridegroom rejoices over a bride.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">יָמִין וּשְׂמֹאל תִּפְרֹֽצִי</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">yamin usemol tifrotsi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Right and left you shall spread out,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וְאֶת יְיָ תַּעֲרִֽיצִי</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">veet Adonai taaritsi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and the Lord you shall revere;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">עַל יַד אִישׁ בֶּן פַּרְצִי</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">al yad ish ben partsi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>by the hand of a man, a son of Perez,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">וְנִשְׂמְחָה וְנָגִֽילָה</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">venismekhah venagilah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and we shall rejoice and be glad.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">בּֽוֹאִי בְשָׁלוֹם עֲטֶֽרֶת בַּעְלָהּ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">boi veshalom ateret balah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Come in peace, crown of her husband,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">גַּם בְּשִׂמְחָה וּבְצָהֳלָה</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">gam besimkhah uvetsaholah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>with joy and with gladness too,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">תּוֹךְ אֱמוּנֵי עַם סְגֻלָּה</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">tokh emune am segullah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>among the faithful of the treasured people;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">בּֽוֹאִי כַלָּה בּֽוֹאִי כַלָּה</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">boi khallah boi khallah</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>come, O bride; come, O bride;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">בּֽוֹאִי כַלָּה שַׁבָּת מַלְכְּתָא.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">boi khallah shabbat malketa</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>come, O bride, Sabbath queen.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Solomon Alkabetz (16th century). Text from <a href="https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%9C%D7%9B%D7%94_%D7%93%D7%95%D7%93%D7%99" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (SBL general-purpose).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (6)</summary><ul><li>The divine name is written יְיָ, an abbreviation read &quot;Adonai&quot; (&quot;the Lord&quot;); it is romanized as it is said.</li><li>line 2.1: the two forms of the Sabbath commandment, &quot;Keep&quot; (Deuteronomy 5:12) and &quot;Remember&quot; (Exodus 20:8), said by tradition to have been spoken at once.</li><li>line 3.3: 'nesukhah' echoes Proverbs 8:23 (Wisdom 'set up' or 'anointed' from of old); the sense is disputed.</li><li>line 5.3 and 9.3: the son of Jesse (David) and the son of Perez (David's ancestor) stand for the Messiah.</li><li>line 10.5: 'malketa', 'queen', is Aramaic.</li><li>line 10.4-5: written כַלָּה without dagesh after a vowel, hence khallah.</li></ul></details></section>
