<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Psalm 19, in Estro Poetico-Armonico (Benedetto Marcello)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1724</span><span class="sg-chain-h">a Lekha Dodi melody used, as an air of the German Jews</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">She Walks in Beauty, in Hebrew Melodies (Isaac Nathan, words by Lord Byron)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1815</span><span class="sg-chain-h">a London Lekha Dodi melody used</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Song of Songs 7:12</span><span class="sg-chain-h">phrase of the refrain</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Tractate Eruvin 21b (Rava's interpretation)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">reading of the Song of Songs as Israel speaking to God</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Melody of a composition by Judah Halevi</span><span class="sg-chain-h">melody used by some Sephardic congregations</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Wezm ja Kontusz, Wezm (Polish folk song)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of the Three Weeks melody, as traced by Eduard Birnbaum</span></li></ul></section>
